WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.334
-Our next guest is an
Emmy-nominated sports journalist

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:05.000
and lead sideline reporter
for "Fox NFL Sunday."

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.167
Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:08.234
our friend Erin Andrews,
everybody!

00:00:08.234 --> 00:00:10.100
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:17.667
♪♪

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:25.234
♪♪

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:28.167
Welcome back!
I am so happy you're here.

00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:31.100
I mean, anyone who watches
football knows that on Sunday

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.767
you were in terrible weather
in Cleveland, Ohio.

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.434
And now look at you.
It's like you --

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:36.734
Had no idea.

00:00:36.734 --> 00:00:38.901
-You know,
a ton of undergarments

00:00:38.901 --> 00:00:40.434
that suck you in.

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:42.601
And the baseball hat's gone.
And there we go. Yeah.

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:44.400
-It was a very --
It was terrible weather,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.100
but it was
a very exciting game and --

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.033
-Let's be honest.
It wasn't that bad of weather.

00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:50.868
I mean, it was on-and-off rain.
I love a weather report.

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:53.033
What would you do
if you weren't doing this?

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:54.801
-You would do the weather?
-I would love to work

00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:56.901
for the Weather Channel
or Fox Weather.

00:00:56.901 --> 00:00:58.467
Yeah.
I would love to do that.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:00.634
I love miles-per-hour
wind gusts.

00:01:00.634 --> 00:01:02.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.934
I love, like, rain throughout.
That wasn't that funny.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:06.200
But I love, like, you know,
the rain throughout the game.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.734
-It wasn't funny.
It was shocking.

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.467
-Oh.
-I don't think anybody's

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:10.868
ever heard someone be like,

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:13.133
"I mean, who doesn't love
miles-per-hour wind gusts?"

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:14.968
-Right.
17-to-18-mile-per-hour winds

00:01:14.968 --> 00:01:16.334
we were dealing with yesterday.

00:01:16.334 --> 00:01:18.067
-I will say
17-to-18-mile-per-hour wind --

00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:20.601
When people say that
on the news, I always wonder,

00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:23.567
"Now what am I supposed to do
with that information?"

00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:25.834
Like, have you ever heard --
At what mile per hour

00:01:25.834 --> 00:01:27.734
are you like,
"Well, I'm scrapping my plans"?

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:29.634
-Well, yesterday 'cause
I had to put a hat on

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:31.901
because, you know, you needed
the hair to be good to go.

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:34.167
-So at what mile per hour
do you go with hat?

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:36.901
-So, 17 to 18.
I thought, did it affect --

00:01:36.901 --> 00:01:38.601
Listen.
We're paying attention.

00:01:38.601 --> 00:01:40.100
-I know. Yes.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.367
-You know, they missed the field
goal and all that stuff.

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:43.868
But the weather
wasn't that crazy.

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:45.601
-Gotcha.
-But it wasn't lovely.

00:01:45.601 --> 00:01:47.534
But it's that time of year.
It's football, baby.

00:01:47.534 --> 00:01:49.367
-You love it, right?
-Steelers.

00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:52.067
-Do -- Steelers are fine.

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:53.934
-They come to LA next week.
-They do come to LA.

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:55.934
And I'll tell you this.
I don't know who's gonna win.

00:01:55.934 --> 00:01:58.467
But I know there will be
more Steelers fans in LA than --

00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:00.567
-Alright. Relax.
-I'm just telling you.

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:02.534
-Have you gone to SoFi yet?
-I haven't gone to SoFi.

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:03.968
-Oh, yeah. It's cool.
-Is it cool?

00:02:03.968 --> 00:02:05.167
The problem is there's just --

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:07.067
You know, LA sports fans
are a little different.

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:08.467
Like, the Steelers fans
bring it.

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:10.667
-Okay. Well, they do.
Hey. One of my best --

00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:13.501
We have -- This was nothing
that I talked about
with your producers.

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:16.667
One of my favorite
Steelers games

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:19.667
was Dallas at Pittsburgh.

00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:22.067
It was Dak and Zeke's,
like, rookie season.

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:23.968
I remember two minutes left.
-You can stop.

00:02:23.968 --> 00:02:26.400
-But you lost.
The towels were going.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.667
All the lint was flying.
It was awesome. Crazy scene.

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:30.334
-By the way, you don't
ever have to tell me

00:02:30.334 --> 00:02:32.868
the ending of a Steelers game.
I have a --

00:02:32.868 --> 00:02:35.901
'Cause we went down
and scored on one of those --

00:02:35.901 --> 00:02:38.634
Roethlisberger was gonna
fake spike. And then touchdown.

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:41.767
You know what? That is a very
famous game in my household.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:43.033
-Why?
-Because that is the first time

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:46.767
my wife said,
"I don't like you after these."

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:50.100
-So, my dad --
massive Red Sox fan.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.701
And I guess my parents'
relationship got a little rocky.

00:02:53.701 --> 00:02:56.367
They're still together.
Love them. Yay! Clap for that.

00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:57.868
[ Applause ]

00:02:57.868 --> 00:02:59.267
But I guess --

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:01.534
You can figure out which
World Series moment it was.

00:03:01.534 --> 00:03:03.667
I was a, like, young baby.

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:06.534
My dad took baby powder
and threw it across the room.

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:08.767
And my mom's like,
"You cannot behave like this."

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:10.167
-Oh, my God.
-Do you have a moment

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:11.901
in your house?
Was it that one?

00:03:11.901 --> 00:03:13.701
-Yes, but here's --
Well, that's --

00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:15.667
So, you're talking about '86,
for sure.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:17.434
So, the one --
the bad one in our house,

00:03:17.434 --> 00:03:19.868
which was 2003,
the Aaron Boone home run.

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:21.634
Now, again, the next year,

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.901
the Red Sox win
and everything's hunky-dory.

00:03:23.901 --> 00:03:25.934
-Yes.
-But my mom is only a sports fan

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:28.534
because of my dad
and her two boys, right?

00:03:28.534 --> 00:03:31.067
And so she's become
a huge sports fan

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:32.501
and she cares
more than any of us.

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:34.801
And when Aaron Boone
hit the home run,

00:03:34.801 --> 00:03:36.901
she stood up and --
according to my dad -- said,

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:40.000
"This is all the fault of you
and those mother [bleep] boys."

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.701
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.701 --> 00:03:44.167
-Of course it is.
It is your fault.

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:46.601
-That's a mind-boggling
sentence to say, though.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:49.567
-Well. Traumatizing.
How's the PTSD from that one?

00:03:49.567 --> 00:03:51.200
-As with everything
in our family,

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.868
we pretend
like it never happened.

00:03:52.868 --> 00:03:55.334
-Yeah, exactly.
-Now, you have a very --

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.300
Obviously,
I'm not breaking news here

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.734
that one of the biggest fans...
-Wow.

00:04:00.734 --> 00:04:04.167
-Usually a fan does not affect
an NFL season like this.

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:05.834
-Right.
-But Taylor Swift, who --

00:04:05.834 --> 00:04:07.167
credit where credit's due --

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:09.801
in August,
you and your podcast host...

00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:11.467
-Yep. Charissa Thompson. Yep.

00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:13.033
-You and Charissa
basically said to --

00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:14.767
You basically threw it out
to the universe,

00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:16.501
and you thought Travis
should go on a date.

00:04:16.501 --> 00:04:18.133
-Have you met him?
-Yes. He's wonderful.

00:04:18.133 --> 00:04:19.634
-He's great. Right?
-He's wonderful.

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:21.167
She's wonderful.
I totally understand

00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:22.868
why you guys thought
this would be a good match.

00:04:22.868 --> 00:04:24.567
-Yeah.
-But even more exciting,

00:04:24.567 --> 00:04:26.367
you have an apparel line,

00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:30.467
and this is sort of NFL gear
for the female fan.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:32.300
-Yeah.
-And she wore it to a game.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.834
Now --
-She wore it last Thursday.

00:04:33.834 --> 00:04:35.367
Yeah. It's our --

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:36.467
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thank you. Yeah.

00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:38.200
This was big, man,
and, you know --

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.133
-I mean, I got to imagine if
you've got an apparel brand...

00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:43.133
-If you have anything
and she puts it on.

00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:45.634
I mean, if you own a Kleenex
company and she wipes her --

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:48.934
it's like -- Oh, my God!
Yes. This was a huge moment.

00:04:48.934 --> 00:04:51.701
And, yeah, in August,
Travis was very open

00:04:51.701 --> 00:04:53.601
about not getting to meet her.

00:04:53.601 --> 00:04:55.901
And so we were just being really
candid because we love him.

00:04:55.901 --> 00:04:57.767
We're good friends of his.
And we just were like,

00:04:57.767 --> 00:05:00.100
"Taylor, date this guy.
I mean, he's good-looking."

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.167
And I can say that
'cause my husband loves him

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:03.868
and they've met before.

00:05:03.868 --> 00:05:06.234
Got to be really careful where
you throw the "good-lookings."

00:05:06.234 --> 00:05:07.801
-Yeah.
-But, yeah, he's just

00:05:07.801 --> 00:05:09.400
such a great, great guy.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.434
And so we threw it out there.
They got together.

00:05:11.434 --> 00:05:13.267
Of course, we're getting
all the credit now

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:14.567
on the "Calm Down" podcast.

00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:17.133
We hope to officiate
the wedding.

00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:19.567
We hope to be there when
he proposes, all the things.

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:22.300
But, yeah, she put the --
she put my jacket on.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.634
-Are you getting calls

00:05:23.634 --> 00:05:26.167
from the warehouse being like,
"We need more staff!"?

00:05:26.167 --> 00:05:27.467
Like, what happens?

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:29.234
-Yes. Yeah. Well, we restocked.

00:05:29.234 --> 00:05:31.267
As soon as she wore it,
I saw it.

00:05:31.267 --> 00:05:33.200
I started screaming
and freaking out

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.567
and singing all
my favorite Swiftie songs.

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:38.634
And then, yeah, we restocked
and we sold out.

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:40.767
And thank you, thank you,
Taylor Swift!

00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:44.567
And now starting today,
we have more up on fanatics.com.

00:05:44.567 --> 00:05:49.000
And your wife, I heard,
has the same impact

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.701
when she wears team apparel.
-She does. Yeah.

00:05:50.701 --> 00:05:52.701
-So you know we're sending you
some Pittsburgh gear.

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:54.334
-That'd be great.
We're gonna get her out there.

00:05:54.334 --> 00:05:56.033
-Flying off the shelves. Yeah.

00:05:56.033 --> 00:05:57.501
-It'd be hard to know
what the spike

00:05:57.501 --> 00:05:59.501
is attributable to, you know?

00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:01.200
Is it Taylor or is it Alexi?
But, you know...

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.868
-Yeah. Exactly.
Well, we'll see. We'll compare.

00:06:02.868 --> 00:06:04.267
I'll let you know.
-You and Charissa --

00:06:04.267 --> 00:06:05.901
You mentioned. I do want
to hammer home again.

00:06:05.901 --> 00:06:07.601
It's really wonderful.
You guys are old friends.

00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:09.701
And "Calm Down" podcast
is a delight.

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:12.133
And it does other things
other than match-make.

00:06:12.133 --> 00:06:14.834
It's a wonderful --
It's a wonderful podcast

00:06:14.834 --> 00:06:16.601
where two friends talk about
what they're experts in.

00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:18.000
And, hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.534
-Thanks for having me.
-It's always such a pleasure.

00:06:19.534 --> 00:06:21.000
Erin Andrews, everybody.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.667
You can catch her every week
on "Fox NFL Sunday."

00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:26.567
And "Calm Down" is available
wherever you get your podcasts.

00:06:26.567 --> 00:06:29.133
Stick around. We'll be back
with music from Ryan Beatty.

